# Regression test for a wrong fix that was tried (and reverted) while fixing the race
# behind echo_stream_is_synchronous.crush: PrettyPrinter::print_value's internal thread
# spawn for a stream value (src/lang/pretty.rs) exists specifically so that printing a
# live or effectively infinite stream doesn't block the calling thread -- e.g. a
# backgrounded `seq | schedule {} | echo &`, where the stream can arrive at one row a
# second or never end. Job::eval's `is_background` branch still dispatches the last
# stage's eval() synchronously before returning (only the *waiting* is skipped, not the
# dispatch), so if `echo` printed a stream synchronously, a backgrounded job ending in
# `echo` of a stream would block the dispatching thread forever instead of actually
# running in the background.
#
# `schedule`'s default initial delay equals its interval, so the very first row isn't
# available for 2 seconds -- comfortably longer than the few milliseconds this script
# needs to reach crush:exit below. If echo's stream printing ever becomes synchronous
# again, dispatching the `&` job blocks for the entire (never-ending) stream instead of
# returning immediately, and this test times out under run_system_test's own 30s limit
# instead of completing in about two seconds.
#
# No echo/golden output needed: crush:exit refuses while any job is still running (see
# the sibling test exit_refuses_while_stream_running.crush), which doubles as
# confirmation that the backgrounded job is genuinely still active, not just fast or
# never started -- if it ever isn't, the first crush:exit below succeeds outright,
# ending the process with the default status 0 rather than ever reaching the
# unconditional crush:exit --force 1 that follows, which the sibling .crush.status file
# checks for.

seq 1 1000001 | schedule $(duration:of seconds=2) | echo &

try {
    crush:exit
} catch {|$e|
}
crush:exit --force 1
